// SV-OS Content Richness Pass — File 2 of 5: Data Structures + Algorithms (15 nodes total). Status: COMPLETE — 15/15 nodes (Data Structures 7, Algorithms 8). Format: JSONL — one compact JSON object per line, UTF-8. Schema: {title, summary, learning_outcomes[3], common_mistakes[3-4], worked_example:{setup, steps[], result}}. Strip this comment line before parsing.
{"title":"Arrays & Strings","summary":"Arrays store elements in contiguous memory, which is what gives O(1) random access — the address of element i is computed directly as base_address + i × element_size, with no traversal needed. This contiguity also makes arrays cache-friendly but costly to resize: a static array has a fixed size, while a dynamic array (like a Python list or Java ArrayList) grows by allocating a new, larger block (typically doubling) and copying existing elements when full — an O(n) operation that happens rarely enough that the amortized cost of appending is still O(1) across a sequence of insertions. Insertion or deletion in the middle of an array is O(n) worst case, since every subsequent element must shift to stay contiguous, which is the key trade-off against linked lists (O(1) insertion/deletion at a known position, O(n) random access). Strings are typically arrays of characters, and in many languages strings are immutable, meaning concatenation creates a new string rather than modifying the original — repeatedly concatenating in a loop is therefore an easy way to accidentally introduce O(n²) behavior, where a mutable buffer would be O(n). Because arrays and strings are the most fundamental data layout in almost every language, understanding their access, insertion, and resizing costs is the baseline every other data structure's trade-offs get compared against.","learning_outcomes":["Given an array-based operation (access by index, insert/delete at a position, append), state its time complexity and justify it in terms of memory layout.","Explain why dynamic array appending is amortized O(1) despite occasional O(n) resize operations, using the doubling strategy's cost accounting.","Identify code that produces accidental O(n²) string-building behavior and rewrite it to achieve O(n) using a mutable buffer or equivalent pattern."],"common_mistakes":["Assuming array insertion/deletion is O(1) like a linked list — because every following element must shift to preserve contiguity, insertion/deletion at an arbitrary position is O(n); only appending at the end (when capacity allows) is O(1).","Treating dynamic array resizing as making every append O(n) — resizing happens rarely (halving in frequency as capacity doubles), so the amortized cost averaged over many appends is O(1).","Concatenating strings inside a loop in a language where strings are immutable — each concatenation allocates and copies a new string, so n concatenations cost O(1+2+...+n) = O(n²), not O(n).","Assuming a 2D array is scattered in memory like a linked structure — in most languages a true 2D array is one contiguous block in row-major order, and index arithmetic computes the offset directly, preserving cache-friendliness."],"worked_example":{"setup":"A dynamic array starts empty with capacity 1 and doubles whenever full. Track total element copies while appending 8 elements one at a time.","steps":["Append 1: capacity 1 fits it, no resize. Copies: 0 (total: 0).","Append 2: capacity 1 is full — resize to 2, copying 1 existing element (total: 1).","Append 3: capacity 2 is full — resize to 4, copying 2 existing elements (total: 3).","Append 4: capacity 4 has room, no resize (total: 3).","Append 5: capacity 4 is full — resize to 8, copying 4 existing elements (total: 7).","Appends 6, 7, 8: capacity 8 has room for each, no resize (total stays 7)."],"result":"Total copies after 8 appends = 7 — well under 2×8=16 — giving under 1 copy per append on average; even though individual resizing appends cost O(n) at that moment, the total cost across all n appends stays O(n), which is exactly what makes the amortized per-append cost O(1)."}}
{"title":"Linked Lists","summary":"A linked list stores elements as individual nodes, each holding a value and a pointer to the next node (and, in a doubly linked list, also a pointer to the previous node), so elements are scattered in memory and connected only through these references rather than being contiguous like an array. This makes insertion and deletion O(1) once you already have a reference to the relevant node, since it only requires updating a constant number of pointers — no shifting is needed, the key advantage over arrays; the trade-off is that random access is O(n), since there's no way to compute a node's location directly and the list must be traversed from the head. A singly linked list can only be traversed forward, while a doubly linked list can be traversed both directions and supports O(1) deletion of a given node without needing a separate reference to its predecessor, at the cost of extra memory per node. Two classic techniques define much of linked-list problem-solving: iterative in-place reversal (re-pointing each node's next pointer to its predecessor while walking through once, O(n) time, O(1) space), and Floyd's cycle detection ('tortoise and hare'), which uses two pointers moving at different speeds — if they ever meet, a cycle exists, provably detecting any cycle in O(n) time with O(1) space, unlike a hash-set approach needing O(n) space. Because linked lists trade random access for cheap insertion/deletion, they underlie other structures, like chaining in hash tables or adjacency lists in graphs, wherever that specific trade-off is useful.","learning_outcomes":["Given a linked-list operation (insert at head/tail/middle, delete a node, traverse), state its time complexity and explain why, referencing whether a node reference is already held.","Implement iterative in-place reversal of a singly linked list and trace the pointer updates for a small example list.","Apply Floyd's cycle detection algorithm to a given linked list (with or without a cycle) and determine whether a cycle exists, tracing pointer positions."],"common_mistakes":["Assuming linked-list insertion/deletion is always O(1) regardless of context — it's O(1) only once a reference to the relevant node is already held; finding that node (e.g. 'delete the value 7') still requires an O(n) traversal.","Forgetting to save a reference before overwriting a node's next pointer during reversal — overwriting it first loses the rest of the list, a common source of a broken chain or accidental cycle.","Assuming a singly linked list gets doubly linked list's O(1)-deletion-of-a-known-node property for free — that optimization specifically requires the prev pointer a doubly linked list provides.","Assuming a hash-set-based cycle detection is strictly better than Floyd's algorithm — the hash-set approach is O(n) time but also O(n) space, while Floyd's achieves the same O(n) time with O(1) space, which matters for large lists or constrained memory."],"worked_example":{"setup":"A linked list is 1→2→3→4→5→3 (node 5 points back to node 3, forming a cycle). Trace Floyd's algorithm (slow moves 1 step, fast moves 2 steps per iteration, both starting at the head).","steps":["Initialize: slow=node1, fast=node1.","Iteration 1: slow moves to node2; fast moves 1→2→3, landing on node3.","Iteration 2: slow moves to node3; fast moves 3→4→5, landing on node5.","Iteration 3: slow moves to node4; fast moves 5→3(cycle edge)→4, landing on node4."],"result":"Slow and fast meet at node4 after 3 iterations, confirming the list contains a cycle — using only two pointers and O(1) extra space, no visited-node storage needed."}}
{"title":"Stacks & Queues","summary":"A stack is a Last-In-First-Out (LIFO) structure supporting push (add to top), pop (remove from top), and peek (view the top), all O(1) when backed by a dynamic array or a linked list with a head pointer — only the most recently added element is accessible. A queue is First-In-First-Out (FIFO), supporting enqueue (add to back) and dequeue (remove from front); a naive array implementation that shifts elements after dequeue is O(n), but O(1) enqueue and dequeue are achievable with a linked list holding head and tail pointers, or a circular buffer (an array wrapping index arithmetic with modulo, avoiding shifts). Stacks and queues model real control flow directly: the call stack every program uses to track function calls is a literal stack (why deep, unbounded recursion causes a stack overflow), and breadth-first search uses a queue to explore level-by-level while depth-first search uses a stack (explicitly, or implicitly via recursion's call stack) to explore as far as possible along a branch before backtracking. Stacks are also the natural tool for matching/balancing problems (balanced parentheses, postfix evaluation, infix-to-postfix conversion) because they let you handle the most recently opened, unmatched item first. A deque (double-ended queue) generalizes both, supporting O(1) insertion and removal at both ends, often used when a problem needs stack-like and queue-like access interchangeably, such as in sliding-window algorithms.","learning_outcomes":["Implement a stack and a queue using both array-based and linked-list-based approaches, and state the time complexity of each operation for each implementation.","Given an infix expression, use a stack to convert it to postfix notation (or evaluate a postfix expression directly), tracing the stack's contents at each step.","Explain why BFS is implemented with a queue and DFS with a stack, and trace which nodes are visited in which order for a small graph under each."],"common_mistakes":["Implementing a queue with a plain array using dequeue-from-front (shifting all remaining elements left) and assuming it's O(1) — the shift makes it O(n); true O(1) needs a linked list with a tail pointer or a circular buffer.","Confusing which structure BFS and DFS use — BFS uses a queue (level-by-level, FIFO), DFS uses a stack (depth-first, LIFO); swapping them changes the traversal order entirely.","Thinking 'DFS uses a stack' only refers to an explicit stack structure — recursive DFS implicitly uses the call stack, so unbounded recursion depth can cause a real stack overflow, the same failure mode as an explicit stack running out of space.","Using a stack where a queue was needed (or vice versa) for order-sensitive problems — the visited-order and correctness of many graph/tree algorithms depend entirely on using the right structure, not just any structure that 'holds things'."],"worked_example":{"setup":"Use a stack to check whether \"{[()()]}\" has balanced brackets, tracing the stack as each character is processed.","steps":["Read '{': push. Stack: [ { ].","Read '[': push. Stack: [ { [ ].","Read '(': push. Stack: [ { [ ( ].","Read ')': pop top '(' — matches. Stack: [ { [ ].","Read '(': push. Stack: [ { [ ( ].","Read ')': pop top '(' — matches. Stack: [ { [ ].","Read ']': pop top '[' — matches. Stack: [ { ].","Read '}': pop top '{' — matches. Stack: [ ] (empty)."],"result":"Every closing bracket matched the most recently opened, unmatched bracket, and the stack ends empty — \"{[()()]}\" is balanced."}}
{"title":"Trees & Binary Search Trees","summary":"A tree is a hierarchical structure of nodes connected by edges with no cycles, rooted at a single top node, where each node except the root has exactly one parent; a binary tree restricts each node to at most two children, called left and right. Trees are traversed in four standard orders: preorder (node, then left, then right), inorder (left, node, right), postorder (left, right, node), and level-order (level by level, left to right, implemented with a queue rather than recursion) — inorder traversal of a binary search tree specifically visits nodes in sorted order, one of the BST's defining practical properties. A Binary Search Tree (BST) maintains that for every node, all values in its left subtree are smaller and all values in its right subtree are larger, making search, insertion, and deletion all O(h), where h is the tree's height — for a balanced tree h is O(log n), giving efficient operations, but a degenerate, skewed tree (e.g. built by inserting already-sorted data with no rebalancing) can degrade h to O(n), making the BST behave like a linked list. Deletion is the trickiest operation: deleting a leaf is trivial, deleting a node with one child splices that child up, but deleting a node with two children requires finding its inorder successor (the smallest value in its right subtree — go right once, then left as far as possible), copying that value into the deleted node's position, and then deleting the successor from its original position (guaranteed to have at most one child). Because plain BSTs can degrade to O(n) height, self-balancing variants (AVL trees, red-black trees) exist specifically to guarantee O(log n) height regardless of insertion order, using rotations to stay balanced after every insertion or deletion.","learning_outcomes":["Given a binary tree, produce its preorder, inorder, postorder, and level-order traversal sequences.","Perform search, insertion, and deletion (including the two-children case using an inorder successor) on a BST, tracing the tree's structure before and after.","Explain why BST operations are O(h) rather than O(log n) in general, and identify an insertion sequence that produces a worst-case skewed tree."],"common_mistakes":["Assuming BST operations are always O(log n) — the true bound is O(h); inserting already-sorted data into a plain BST with no rebalancing produces a completely skewed tree with h=O(n).","Deleting a two-children node by removing it and reattaching children arbitrarily — this breaks the BST invariant; the correct approach replaces the node's value with its inorder successor (or predecessor) and removes that node from its original position.","Confusing preorder and postorder — preorder visits the node before its subtrees, postorder visits it after; swapping them changes which node appears first versus last in the output.","Assuming inorder traversal produces sorted output for any binary tree — this guarantee holds specifically for a valid BST because it relies on the left-smaller/right-larger invariant; an arbitrary non-BST binary tree has no such guarantee."],"worked_example":{"setup":"Insert 50, 30, 70, 20, 40, 60, 80 in order into a BST, then delete 50 (the root, which has two children).","steps":["Build the tree: 50 is root; 30 and 70 become its left/right children; 20 and 40 become 30's left/right children; 60 and 80 become 70's left/right children.","To delete 50, find its inorder successor: go right once to 70, then left as far as possible — 70's left child is 60, which has no left child, so 60 is the successor.","Copy the successor's value into the root: root's value becomes 60.","Delete the original node containing 60 (70's left child); since it's a leaf, it's simply removed, so 70's left pointer becomes empty."],"result":"The tree's root becomes 60, with left subtree unchanged (30, with children 20 and 40) and right subtree now just 70 with a single right child 80 — the BST invariant holds at every node."}}
{"title":"Heaps & Priority Queues","summary":"A binary heap is a complete binary tree (every level fully filled except possibly the last, which fills left to right) satisfying the heap property: in a min-heap, every parent is less than or equal to both children (so the minimum is always at the root); a max-heap is the mirror image. Because a heap is complete, it can be stored compactly in a plain array with no pointers — for a node at 0-indexed position i, its children are at 2i+1 and 2i+2, and its parent is at (i−1)/2 (integer division), making heaps memory-efficient compared to a pointer-based tree. Insertion adds the new element at the next open slot and 'sifts up' — repeatedly swapping with its parent while the heap property is violated — O(log n) since it moves at most the tree's height; extraction of the min (or max) removes the root, moves the last array element into the root position, and 'sifts down' — swapping with the smaller (or larger) child while violated — also O(log n). Although inserting n elements one at a time costs O(n log n) total, building a heap from an already-filled array can be done in O(n) using bottom-up heapify (calling sift-down on every non-leaf node from the last one up to the root), because most nodes in a heap are near the bottom and only sift a short distance — a tighter bound than the naive O(n log n) estimate. A priority queue is the abstract data type a heap most commonly implements, underlying algorithms like heapsort, Dijkstra's shortest path (always processing the next-closest unvisited node), and efficiently finding the k-th largest/smallest element in a stream.","learning_outcomes":["Given an array, determine the parent/child index relationships and identify whether the array satisfies the min-heap (or max-heap) property.","Perform insertion (sift-up) and extraction (sift-down) on a heap represented as an array, tracing the array's contents at each swap.","Explain why bottom-up build-heap runs in O(n) rather than the naive O(n log n) estimate, referencing the distribution of node heights in a complete binary tree."],"common_mistakes":["Assuming a heap is fully sorted because the root is always the min (or max) — only root-to-child ordering is guaranteed; siblings have no guaranteed order, so a heap's array is not a sorted array.","Confusing 0-indexed and 1-indexed child/parent formulas — for 0-indexed arrays children are at 2i+1 and 2i+2 (not 2i and 2i+1), and using the wrong formula silently corrupts the heap structure.","Assuming n individual insertions is as fast as bottom-up heapify — both produce a valid heap, but repeated insertion is O(n log n) total while bottom-up heapify is O(n).","Sifting down only once instead of repeatedly during extraction — a single swap with the smaller child isn't enough if the element still violates the heap property further down; sifting must continue until the property holds at every level it passes through."],"worked_example":{"setup":"Build a min-heap from [9, 4, 7, 1, 3, 8] using bottom-up heapify, starting sift-down from the last non-leaf node (index 2 for n=6).","steps":["Index the array: [9,4,7,1,3,8]. Last non-leaf node is at index ⌊6/2⌋−1 = 2.","Sift-down at index2 (value 7): its only child is index5=8; since 7≤8, no swap needed.","Sift-down at index1 (value 4): children are index3=1, index4=3; smaller is index3=1. Since 4>1, swap: array becomes [9,1,7,4,3,8]. Index3 is now a leaf, sifting stops.","Sift-down at index0 (value 9): children are index1=1, index2=7; smaller is index1=1. Since 9>1, swap: [1,9,7,4,3,8]. Continue at index1 (now 9): children index3=4, index4=3; smaller is index4=3. Since 9>3, swap: [1,3,7,4,9,8]. Index4 is now a leaf, sifting stops."],"result":"Heapified array: [1,3,7,4,9,8] — every parent ≤ its children (1≤3, 1≤7, 3≤4, 3≤9, 7≤8), confirming a valid min-heap built in one O(n) bottom-up pass rather than n individual O(log n) insertions."}}
{"title":"Hash Tables","summary":"A hash table maps keys to values using a hash function to convert a key into an array index, giving average-case O(1) insertion, lookup, and deletion — dramatically faster than a linked structure's O(n) search — but only when the hash function distributes keys roughly uniformly and collisions (different keys hashing to the same index) are handled well. Chaining resolves collisions by storing a small linked list at each array slot, so multiple keys hashing to the same index live in that slot's list; open addressing instead probes for a different empty slot within the same array — linear probing checks the next slot sequentially (simple, but prone to clustering), quadratic probing checks slots at increasing quadratic offsets to reduce clustering, and double hashing uses a second hash function for the probe step size, spreading collisions more evenly. The load factor (elements stored divided by table size) is the key variable controlling performance: as it approaches 1, average operation cost degrades, so hash tables resize — typically doubling capacity and rehashing every element — once the load factor crosses a threshold (often around 0.7), keeping amortized operation cost O(1) the same way dynamic array doubling does. Worst-case lookup is O(n), which happens if many keys collide into the same slot, either from a poorly chosen hash function or deliberately crafted colliding keys in adversarial settings — this gap between average and worst case is why hash table guarantees are usually stated probabilistically. Hash tables underlie associative arrays/dictionaries/maps in virtually every language's standard library, and are also the basis of memoization in dynamic programming and de-duplication of large datasets.","learning_outcomes":["Given a hash function and a sequence of keys, insert them into a hash table using chaining and trace the resulting bucket contents, including any collisions.","Insert the same sequence of keys using open addressing with linear probing, and trace the resulting array and probe sequences, contrasting with the chaining result.","Explain why average-case hash table operations are O(1) while worst-case is O(n), and identify what property of the hash function and load factor drives each case."],"common_mistakes":["Assuming hash table lookup is always O(1) regardless of the hash function or data — this holds only under a reasonably uniform hash function and controlled load factor; a poor function or adversarial keys can force O(n) worst case.","Forgetting clustering in linear probing — because it fills consecutive slots, once a cluster forms, every new key hashing near it extends the cluster further, degrading performance faster than load factor alone suggests.","Not resizing the table as the load factor grows — without resizing, collision chains (or probe sequences) grow steadily longer, silently degrading what should be O(1) operations toward O(n).","Confusing the hash function with the collision resolution strategy — the hash function maps a key to an initial index, while collision resolution decides what happens when two keys land on the same index; a good hash function reduces collisions but doesn't eliminate the need for a resolution strategy."],"worked_example":{"setup":"Using a hash table with 5 slots (0-4) and h(key) = key mod 5, insert keys 12, 7, 17, 3, 22 in order using chaining.","steps":["h(12)=12 mod5=2. Insert into bucket 2. Buckets: {2:[12]}.","h(7)=7 mod5=2. Collision — chain onto bucket 2. Buckets: {2:[12,7]}.","h(17)=17 mod5=2. Collision — chain on. Buckets: {2:[12,7,17]}.","h(3)=3 mod5=3. Bucket 3 empty — insert directly. Buckets: {2:[12,7,17], 3:[3]}.","h(22)=22 mod5=2. Collision — chain on. Buckets: {2:[12,7,17,22], 3:[3]}."],"result":"Bucket 2 ends up holding 4 of the 5 keys (all ≡2 mod5) while bucket 3 holds 1, and buckets 0,1,4 stay empty — a concrete illustration of how keys sharing a residue degrade average-case O(1) toward the O(n) worst case for that bucket, even with a reasonable overall load factor (5 keys, 5 slots)."}}
{"title":"Advanced Trees (B-Tree, Trie, Segment Tree)","summary":"This topic covers three specialized tree structures, each solving a different problem plain binary trees handle poorly. A B-tree is a self-balancing, multi-way tree (each node can have many children) designed for systems where reading from disk is expensive — by keeping many keys per node and a large branching factor, a B-tree keeps its height very small (typically just 3-4 levels even for millions of keys), minimizing disk reads per search, which is why B-trees (and the closely related B+ tree) are the standard structure behind database indexes and filesystem metadata; every B-tree also guarantees all leaves sit at the same depth, keeping operations O(log n) rather than risking the skew a plain BST can suffer. A Trie (prefix tree) stores strings character by character along root-to-node paths rather than as whole-string keys, so every node represents a shared prefix, and looking up, inserting, or checking whether a string exists takes O(L) time where L is the string's length, independent of how many other strings are stored — well suited for autocomplete and prefix-matching, since all strings sharing a prefix live under the same subtree. A Segment Tree is built over an array to answer range queries (range sum, min, or max) and support point or range updates, both in O(log n): each node represents the aggregate of a contiguous range, with leaves as individual elements and each internal node derived from combining its two children, so both querying an arbitrary range and updating a single element touch only O(log n) nodes along the relevant paths. All three trade the simplicity of a plain binary tree for a specific guarantee — B-trees guarantee balance under disk-oriented constraints, tries guarantee length-proportional string operations, and segment trees guarantee logarithmic range queries — so choosing the right one depends on matching the guarantee to the problem's actual access pattern.","learning_outcomes":["Explain why B-trees are optimized for disk-based storage specifically, connecting branching factor and tree height to the number of disk reads a search requires.","Insert a small set of strings into a trie and trace which nodes are shared (common prefixes) versus newly created.","Build a segment tree over a small array for range-sum queries, and trace a range query and a point update, identifying which nodes are visited."],"common_mistakes":["Assuming a B-tree is just 'a binary tree with a different name' — a B-tree node holds multiple keys and can have many children, and this wide branching factor is specifically what keeps height small for disk-based access.","Underestimating a trie's memory cost — even though operations are fast (O(L)), each node can need space for many possible next characters, so a trie storing few strings with little shared prefix structure can use considerably more memory than a simple hash set.","Assuming a segment tree query touches only O(1) nodes because 'it's just reading a precomputed aggregate' — an arbitrary range query generally needs to combine O(log n) partial-range nodes, since the queried range rarely aligns exactly with one node's stored range.","Confusing a segment tree with a simple prefix-sum array — a prefix-sum array answers range-sum queries in O(1) but needs O(n) to update after any element changes, while a segment tree answers both range queries and point updates in O(log n)."],"worked_example":{"setup":"Build a segment tree over [2,4,5,7,8,9] for range-sum queries, then query the sum of range [1,4] (0-indexed, inclusive — elements 4,5,7,8).","steps":["Build recursively: leaf nodes hold individual elements; node [0,2] (indices 0-2) sums to 2+4+5=11; node [3,5] (indices 3-5) sums to 7+8+9=24; root [0,5] holds 35.","Query [1,4] from root [0,5]: not fully inside or outside [1,4], so recurse into both children.","Left child [0,2] also partially overlaps: its sub-child [0,1] splits into leaf [0,0]=2 (fully outside [1,4], contributes 0) and leaf [1,1]=4 (fully inside, contributes 4); sibling [2,2]=5 is fully inside, contributing 5. Left side total: 0+4+5=9.","Right child [3,5] partially overlaps: its sub-child [3,4] (sum 7+8=15) is fully inside [1,4], contributing 15 directly; sibling [5,5]=9 is fully outside, contributing 0. Right side total: 15."],"result":"Query [1,4] returns 9+15=24, matching 4+5+7+8=24 computed directly — and the query touched only a handful of the tree's nodes rather than scanning all 6 elements, the O(log n) pattern that lets segment trees answer range queries without a full array scan."}}
{"title":"Complexity Analysis (Big-O)","summary":"Complexity analysis measures how an algorithm's resource usage (usually time, sometimes space) grows as input size n grows, using asymptotic notation that deliberately ignores constant factors and lower-order terms to focus on growth rate at scale. Big-O (O) gives an asymptotic upper bound — f(n)=O(g(n)) means there exist constants c>0 and n₀ such that f(n)≤c·g(n) for all n≥n₀; Big-Omega (Ω) is the mirror-image lower bound, and Big-Theta (Θ) requires both, giving a tight bound on growth rate. Analyzing code means counting how the dominant operations scale: a single loop over n elements is O(n), nested loops over n elements each are O(n²), and a loop that halves its range each iteration (like binary search) is O(log n); for recursive algorithms, the recurrence relation (e.g., T(n)=2T(n/2)+O(n) for merge sort, which solves to O(n log n)) must be solved, commonly via the Master Theorem, to get the closed-form complexity. An algorithm's complexity can also depend on which specific input is given, which is why best-case, worst-case, and average-case are distinguished — quicksort, for instance, is O(n log n) on average but O(n²) in the worst case (e.g. already-sorted input with a naive pivot choice), and while Big-O can technically describe any of the three, it's often used loosely to mean worst-case unless the case is stated explicitly. Understanding complexity analysis is what lets you compare two algorithms solving the same problem without running both, and predict how runtime will actually scale as real input sizes grow far larger than what you could test by hand.","learning_outcomes":["Given a code snippet (loops, nested loops, or simple recursion), derive its Big-O time complexity by analyzing the dominant operation count.","State and apply the formal definition of Big-O (or Big-Omega/Big-Theta) to verify or disprove a claimed asymptotic bound for a small function.","Distinguish an algorithm's best-case, worst-case, and average-case complexity, given a concrete example (like quicksort) where they differ."],"common_mistakes":["Treating O(n) and Θ(n) as interchangeable — O(n) is only an upper bound (an algorithm that's actually O(1) is technically also O(n)), while Θ(n) asserts a tight bound; using O where Θ is meant overstates uncertainty about the true growth rate.","Adding the complexities of sequential (non-nested) code blocks instead of taking the maximum — a O(n) loop followed by a separate O(n²) loop combine to O(n²) overall, not O(n³); only nested loops multiply.","Assuming a recursive algorithm's complexity can be read off from the recursive calls alone, without accounting for the work done outside them at each level — merge sort's O(n) merge step at every level is what makes it O(n log n), not O(log n).","Quoting an algorithm's average-case complexity as if it were a guarantee — quicksort's O(n log n) is a typical-case figure; a poor pivot-selection strategy against adversarial or already-sorted input can still trigger the O(n²) worst case."],"worked_example":{"setup":"Solve the recurrence T(n) = 2T(n/2) + n (the merge sort recurrence) using the recursion-tree method, and confirm with the Master Theorem.","steps":["Draw the recursion tree: the top level does n work outside its 2 recursive calls of size n/2 each.","The next level has 2 subproblems of size n/2, each doing n/2 work: total work at this level = 2×(n/2) = n.","The next level has 4 subproblems of size n/4, each doing n/4 work: total = 4×(n/4) = n. Every level does exactly n total work.","The tree has log₂(n) levels (problem size halves each level, from n down to 1), so total work = n × log₂(n) = O(n log n).","Confirm via Master Theorem: for T(n)=aT(n/b)+f(n) with a=2,b=2,f(n)=n, compute n^(log_b a) = n^(log₂2) = n. Since f(n)=Θ(n^(log_b a)), this is Case 2, giving T(n)=Θ(n^(log_b a)·log n)=Θ(n log n)."],"result":"Both methods agree: T(n)=2T(n/2)+n solves to Θ(n log n) — matching merge sort's well-known complexity, confirmed independently by summing per-level work and by the Master Theorem's Case 2."}}
{"title":"Sorting Algorithms","summary":"Sorting algorithms rearrange elements into order, and the central trade-off is between simplicity/space and time complexity. The simple comparison-based sorts — bubble sort, insertion sort, selection sort — are all O(n²) in the worst case, but insertion sort is efficient for nearly-sorted data (close to O(n) when few elements are out of place) and, along with bubble sort, is stable (preserves the relative order of equal elements), a property not every fast sort has. The efficient comparison-based sorts run in O(n log n): merge sort divides the array in half, recursively sorts each half, and merges in O(n) — stable, with guaranteed O(n log n) in all cases, but needing O(n) extra space; quicksort picks a pivot, partitions around it, and recursively sorts each partition — typically faster in practice (in-place, good cache behavior) but with worst-case O(n²) if the pivot choice is consistently bad, and it is not stable; heapsort builds a max-heap and repeatedly extracts the maximum, guaranteeing O(n log n) worst-case with O(1) extra space, but worse practical cache performance than quicksort and no stability. No comparison-based sort can do better than Ω(n log n) in the worst case — a proven lower bound from a decision-tree argument (n! possible orderings, and each comparison rules out at most half the remaining possibilities, requiring at least log₂(n!)=Θ(n log n) comparisons) — which is why non-comparison sorts like counting sort (O(n+k) for k distinct integer keys) and radix sort (O(d(n+k)) for d digits) can beat n log n, but only by exploiting structure in the keys rather than comparing arbitrary elements.","learning_outcomes":["Given an array, trace the execution of insertion sort, merge sort, and quicksort step by step, showing intermediate array states.","State the time complexity (best, worst, average) and space complexity of a named sorting algorithm, and identify whether it is stable and/or in-place.","Explain the Ω(n log n) lower bound for comparison-based sorting using the decision-tree argument, and identify why a non-comparison sort like counting sort can beat it."],"common_mistakes":["Assuming quicksort's worst-case complexity is O(n log n) because it's usually fast in practice — a consistently poor pivot choice (e.g. first element on sorted input) degrades it to O(n²).","Assuming all O(n log n) sorts are interchangeable — merge sort guarantees O(n log n) always and is stable but needs O(n) space, while heapsort guarantees O(n log n) with O(1) space but isn't stable; the right choice depends on which property the situation needs.","Believing a faster sorting algorithm always exists for any array of comparable elements — the Ω(n log n) lower bound is proven for comparison sorts, so only exploiting extra structure (like small-range integer keys) allows counting or radix sort to beat it.","Assuming 'in-place' always means literally zero extra memory — some definitions allow O(log n) auxiliary space (e.g. recursion stack), so an 'in-place' claim should be checked against exactly how much extra space is used, not assumed to mean none."],"worked_example":{"setup":"Partition [8, 3, 5, 1, 9, 2] using Lomuto partitioning with the last element (2) as the pivot.","steps":["Pivot = 2 (last element). Initialize boundary index i = −1.","j=0 (8): 8≤2? No. j=1 (3): 3≤2? No. j=2 (5): 5≤2? No.","j=3 (1): 1≤2? Yes — increment i to 0, swap arr[0] and arr[3]: array becomes [1, 3, 5, 8, 9, 2].","j=4 (9): 9≤2? No.","Final step: swap arr[i+1]=arr[1] with the pivot at arr[5]: array becomes [1, 2, 5, 8, 9, 3]."],"result":"Pivot (2) lands at index 1: everything before it ([1]) is ≤2, everything after it ([5,8,9,3]) is >2 — a correctly partitioned array, ready for quicksort to recurse independently on each side."}}
{"title":"Searching Algorithms","summary":"Searching algorithms locate a target value within a collection, and the fundamental trade-off is between the precondition required and the resulting speed. Linear search checks each element in sequence, requiring no precondition on the data's order and running in O(n) worst and average case — it's the only option for unsorted data. Binary search instead requires sorted data, and repeatedly halves the search range: compare the target to the middle element, recurse into the left half if smaller or the right half if larger, discarding the other half each step, giving O(log n) time — doubling the input size only adds one more comparison rather than doubling the work. Binary search's precondition is exactly what makes it fast, generalizing to a wider principle: many O(log n) algorithms work by safely discarding a constant fraction of the remaining search space each step, requiring some structural guarantee (sortedness, a heap property) to know which part is safe to discard. Implementing binary search correctly is notoriously easy to get subtly wrong — off-by-one errors in loop bounds, or the midpoint calculation (mid = low + (high−low)/2 is preferred over (low+high)/2 to avoid integer overflow for very large indices), are a common source of bugs, and variants like finding the first or last occurrence of a duplicate value require carefully adjusting which half is discarded when the target is found, rather than returning immediately.","learning_outcomes":["Implement binary search iteratively or recursively on a sorted array, correctly handling loop bounds and midpoint calculation to avoid off-by-one errors.","Explain why binary search requires sorted data and derive its O(log n) complexity from the halving process.","Modify standard binary search to find the first or last occurrence of a target value that appears multiple times in a sorted array."],"common_mistakes":["Running binary search on unsorted data — correctness depends entirely on the sortedness precondition; on unsorted data it can silently return a wrong answer rather than raising an error.","Computing the midpoint as (low+high)/2 instead of low+(high−low)/2 — the former can overflow for very large index values in fixed-width integer types, silently breaking the search.","Returning immediately upon finding a match when duplicates exist and a first/last occurrence is required — the search must instead continue narrowing toward the correct boundary rather than stopping at the first match.","Getting the loop/recursion boundary condition wrong (< instead of <=, or misupdating low/high), causing an infinite loop or skipping the last element — a classic off-by-one source even for experienced programmers."],"worked_example":{"setup":"Find the first occurrence of 5 in the sorted array [1, 3, 5, 5, 5, 7, 9] (0-indexed) using a first-occurrence binary search variant.","steps":["Initialize low=0, high=6, result=−1.","Iteration 1: mid=0+(6−0)/2=3. arr[3]=5 — match, record result=3, keep searching left: high=2.","Iteration 2: low=0, high=2, mid=1. arr[1]=3 < 5 — search right: low=2.","Iteration 3: low=2, high=2, mid=2. arr[2]=5 — match, update result=2 (earlier than 3), keep searching left: high=1.","Now low=2 > high=1, loop ends."],"result":"First occurrence of 5 is at index 2 — found by not stopping at the first match (index 3) but continuing to search the left half for an earlier one."}}
{"title":"Recursion & Divide-and-Conquer","summary":"Recursion is a technique where a function solves a problem by calling itself on a smaller version of the same problem, requiring a base case (the smallest instance, solved directly, which stops the recursion) and a recursive case (which reduces the problem toward the base case) — every call adds a frame to the call stack, which is why recursion without a reachable base case causes unbounded growth and a stack overflow. Divide-and-conquer is a specific recursive strategy with three steps: divide the problem into smaller subproblems of the same type, conquer each recursively (or directly if small enough), and combine the subproblems' solutions — merge sort is the canonical example, dividing the array in half, recursively sorting each half, and combining via an O(n) merge. The efficiency of a divide-and-conquer algorithm depends on the combine step's cost relative to how much smaller subproblems get: merge sort's O(n) combine across O(log n) levels gives O(n log n) overall, while Karatsuba's multiplication reduces the number of subproblems from the naive 4 to 3 per level, lowering complexity from the naive O(n²) to roughly O(n^1.585) — illustrating that the benefit comes from a genuinely favorable recurrence, not merely from recursing. Not every problem benefits from divide-and-conquer restructuring — if the combine step is as expensive as solving the problem directly, the recursive structure adds overhead without a complexity improvement, which is why the choice between iterative and recursive formulations requires analyzing the actual recurrence, not assuming recursion is inherently more efficient. Recursive thinking is also the natural fit for problems with self-similar structure, like tree traversal or problems defined recursively in the first place (Fibonacci, factorial), even when the resulting complexity doesn't beat an iterative approach.","learning_outcomes":["Given a recursive function, identify its base case and recursive case, and trace the call stack (including return values) for a small input.","Apply the divide-and-conquer paradigm (divide, conquer, combine) to decompose a problem, and write the corresponding recurrence relation for its time complexity.","Explain why Karatsuba's multiplication is asymptotically faster than the naive approach, referencing how many recursive subproblems each uses."],"common_mistakes":["Writing a recursive function with no reachable base case — this causes unbounded recursion and a stack overflow rather than a wrong answer, which can make the bug harder to diagnose from output alone.","Assuming any recursive restructuring automatically improves complexity — the improvement depends on the specific recurrence; if the combine step is as expensive as solving directly, recursion adds overhead without an asymptotic benefit.","Recomputing the same subproblem multiple times in a naive recursive solution (like plain recursive Fibonacci) without recognizing this as a sign memoization or DP is needed instead.","Confusing the number of recursive calls with the number of subproblems needing separate solving — Karatsuba's insight is algebraic (deriving the missing cross term from values already computed), not simply doing less work per subproblem."],"worked_example":{"setup":"Trace the recursive call tree for computing Fibonacci(5) via F(n)=F(n−1)+F(n−2), base cases F(0)=0, F(1)=1, and count total function calls.","steps":["F(5) calls F(4) and F(3).","F(4) calls F(3) and F(2); this F(3) calls F(2) and F(1); this F(2) calls base cases F(1) and F(0).","F(4)'s F(2) branch calls base cases F(1) and F(0) again.","F(5)'s other branch, F(3), calls F(2) and F(1); this F(2) calls base cases F(1) and F(0) again.","Counting every call made across the whole tree gives 15 total calls."],"result":"Computing F(5) naively makes 15 calls to solve only 6 distinct subproblems (F(0) through F(5)) — F(3) is solved 2 separate times, F(2) 3 times, F(1) 5 times — the exact redundancy that memoization/DP eliminates."}}
{"title":"Dynamic Programming","summary":"Dynamic programming (DP) solves problems by breaking them into overlapping subproblems and storing each subproblem's solution the first time it's computed, so later calls needing the same subproblem retrieve the stored answer instead of recomputing it — this works when a problem has optimal substructure (an optimal solution can be built from optimal solutions to its subproblems) and overlapping subproblems (the same subproblems recur many times, unlike plain divide-and-conquer, where subproblems are typically distinct). There are two equivalent implementation styles: memoization (top-down) adds a cache to a natural recursive solution, checking the cache before recursing and storing the result after; tabulation (bottom-up) builds a table iteratively from the smallest subproblems up, avoiding recursive call overhead and often making space easier to optimize, since older entries can sometimes be discarded once unneeded. Applying DP to naive recursive Fibonacci (which makes an exponential number of calls, as seen with F(5)'s 15 calls solving only 6 distinct subproblems) reduces it to O(n) time, since each of the n distinct subproblems is now solved exactly once. The general recipe for designing a DP solution is identifying the subproblem definition (what a table entry represents), the recurrence (how a subproblem relates to smaller ones), the base cases, and the computation order (which must respect the recurrence's dependencies) — classic problems like 0/1 knapsack, longest common subsequence, and edit distance are all solved by correctly identifying these four pieces. DP's core value is converting exponential naive recursive complexity into polynomial time by ensuring each distinct subproblem is solved exactly once — complexity becomes (number of distinct subproblems) × (work per subproblem), which is why counting distinct subproblems is the first step in analyzing a DP algorithm.","learning_outcomes":["Given a recursive problem with overlapping subproblems, identify the subproblem definition, recurrence relation, base cases, and correct computation order needed to design a DP solution.","Convert a naive exponential recursive solution into a memoized (top-down) version by adding a cache, and state the resulting time complexity.","Implement a bottom-up (tabulation) DP solution for a small classic problem (such as 0/1 knapsack or longest common subsequence), tracing the table's values."],"common_mistakes":["Applying DP to a problem with optimal substructure but no overlapping subproblems — if subproblems don't actually recur (as in standard merge sort), memoization adds caching overhead without eliminating any redundant work.","Getting the computation order wrong in a bottom-up solution — a table entry must be computed only after every smaller subproblem it depends on is filled in; wrong order reads uninitialized or stale values.","Assuming memoization and tabulation always have the same space complexity — tabulation often allows discarding old entries once unneeded, which can reduce space below what a memoized recursive solution (full cache plus call stack) typically uses.","Defining base cases imprecisely or inconsistently with the recurrence — an incorrect base case propagates errors through the entire table, since later entries build on earlier ones."],"worked_example":{"setup":"Compute F(6) using bottom-up tabulation, storing each F(i) as it's computed.","steps":["Initialize base cases: table[0]=0, table[1]=1.","table[2] = table[1]+table[0] = 1+0 = 1.","table[3] = table[2]+table[1] = 1+1 = 2.","table[4] = table[3]+table[2] = 2+1 = 3.","table[5] = table[4]+table[3] = 3+2 = 5.","table[6] = table[5]+table[4] = 5+3 = 8."],"result":"F(6) = 8, computed with exactly 5 additions (one per entry from index 2 through 6) — a stark contrast to naive recursion's exponential blow-up (which needed 15 calls for the smaller F(5)), confirming DP turns overlapping-subproblem recursion into one computation per distinct subproblem."}}
{"title":"Greedy Algorithms","summary":"A greedy algorithm builds a solution by making the locally optimal choice at each step, never reconsidering it later, which makes greedy algorithms typically simple and fast (often O(n log n), dominated by an initial sort) — but greedy only produces a globally optimal solution for problems with the greedy-choice property (a locally optimal choice is always part of some globally optimal solution) in addition to optimal substructure; proving a greedy strategy correct generally requires an exchange argument, showing any optimal solution can be transformed to include the greedy choice without making it worse. Activity selection (choosing the maximum number of non-overlapping intervals) is a classic example where greedy provably works: always picking the next activity with the earliest finish time is optimal, provable because any solution not containing that choice can be modified to include it without reducing the total count. Huffman coding, building an optimal prefix-free binary code for compression, is another classic case: repeatedly merging the two lowest-frequency nodes is greedy, and is provably optimal for minimizing expected code length. In contrast, the 0/1 knapsack problem (each item taken whole or not at all) is the canonical counterexample: choosing items by best value-to-weight ratio first does not always yield the optimal total value once items can't be split (unlike fractional knapsack, where the same ratio strategy is provably optimal) — which is exactly why every greedy algorithm needs its own correctness proof rather than being assumed correct by analogy. Because greedy is fast but not universally applicable, the practical skill is recognizing which problems have the exchange-argument structure that makes greedy provably correct, rather than applying it reflexively.","learning_outcomes":["Given a problem, determine whether a greedy strategy is likely to work by checking for the greedy-choice property and optimal substructure, or identify why a specific greedy strategy fails using a counterexample.","Apply the earliest-finish-time greedy strategy to solve an activity selection problem, tracing which activities are selected.","Construct a Huffman code for a small set of symbol frequencies, tracing the repeated merging of lowest-frequency nodes."],"common_mistakes":["Assuming a greedy strategy that works for one problem automatically works for a similar-looking one — fractional knapsack's greedy ratio strategy is optimal, but the same choice fails on 0/1 knapsack once items can't be split.","Applying greedy without proving the exchange argument, then treating a locally-plausible answer as necessarily globally optimal — a strategy that seems reasonable can produce a demonstrably suboptimal total without a specific correctness proof.","Sorting by the wrong criterion — activity selection requires sorting by finish time, not start time or duration; a plausible-seeming but incorrect key produces a strategy that looks greedy but isn't provably correct.","Assuming Huffman's greedy merge order doesn't matter when frequencies tie — the resulting expected length is still optimal regardless of tie-breaking, but the actual code produced can differ, which matters if a reproducible encoding is required."],"worked_example":{"setup":"Build a Huffman code for symbols with frequencies A:5, B:9, C:12, D:13, E:16, F:45, repeatedly merging the two lowest-frequency nodes.","steps":["Merge A(5) and B(9) → AB:14. Remaining: AB:14, C:12, D:13, E:16, F:45.","Merge C(12) and D(13) → CD:25. Remaining: AB:14, CD:25, E:16, F:45.","Merge AB(14) and E(16) → ABE:30. Remaining: CD:25, ABE:30, F:45.","Merge CD(25) and ABE(30) → CDABE:55. Remaining: CDABE:55, F:45.","Merge F(45) and CDABE(55) → root:100."],"result":"The tree assigns the shortest code (1 bit) to F, the most frequent symbol, and longer codes (3 bits for C,D,E; 4 bits for A,B) to less frequent ones — always merging the two cheapest options minimizes total expected code length (Σ frequency×depth = 224 for this set)."}}
{"title":"Graph Algorithms","summary":"Graphs model pairwise relationships between entities as vertices connected by edges, represented either as an adjacency matrix (a V×V grid giving O(1) edge lookup but O(V²) space regardless of sparsity) or an adjacency list (each vertex stores its neighbors, giving O(V+E) space, better for sparse graphs, but O(degree) edge lookup). Breadth-first search (BFS) explores level by level using a queue, visiting all neighbors of the current node before going deeper, and is the standard way to find shortest paths in an unweighted graph, running in O(V+E); depth-first search (DFS) explores as far as possible along each branch before backtracking, using a stack (explicit or via recursion), also O(V+E), and underlies cycle detection, topological sorting, and finding connected components. For weighted graphs, Dijkstra's algorithm finds shortest paths from a single source, provided all edge weights are non-negative, by repeatedly selecting the closest unvisited vertex and relaxing its outgoing edges — with a binary heap as the priority queue, this runs in O((V+E) log V); if edges can be negative, Dijkstra's greedy assumption breaks, and Bellman-Ford is used instead, running in O(VE) by relaxing every edge V−1 times, additionally detecting negative-weight cycles as a byproduct. For a Minimum Spanning Tree (minimum-weight edge subset connecting all vertices with no cycles), Kruskal's algorithm greedily adds the smallest-weight edge that doesn't create a cycle (using union-find, giving O(E log E), dominated by sorting), while Prim's algorithm grows a single tree by repeatedly adding the cheapest edge connecting it to a new vertex, with the same O((V+E) log V) complexity as Dijkstra when using a binary heap.","learning_outcomes":["Choose an appropriate graph representation (adjacency matrix vs. adjacency list) for a given graph and use-case, justifying by space and edge-lookup complexity.","Trace BFS and DFS traversal order on a small graph, and use BFS to find the shortest path (in edge count) between two vertices in an unweighted graph.","Trace Dijkstra's algorithm on a small weighted graph with non-negative edges, showing shortest-distance estimates updating at each step."],"common_mistakes":["Applying Dijkstra's algorithm to a graph with negative edge weights — its greedy correctness relies on a finalized vertex's distance never improving later, which negative weights can violate; Bellman-Ford must be used instead.","Confusing BFS and DFS on which guarantees shortest path in an unweighted graph — only BFS (level by level) guarantees the first time a vertex is reached is via a shortest path; DFS gives no such guarantee.","Using an adjacency matrix for a large, sparse graph — the O(V²) space is wasted when most vertex pairs have no edge; an adjacency list's O(V+E) is far more efficient for sparse graphs.","Assuming Kruskal's and Prim's always produce the identical MST — with distinct edge weights the MST is unique, but if weights tie, the two algorithms (or different tie-breaking) can produce different, equally-minimal spanning trees."],"worked_example":{"setup":"Find shortest distances from source A in a graph with edges A-B(4), A-C(1), C-B(2), B-D(5), C-D(8), using Dijkstra's algorithm.","steps":["Initialize dist[A]=0, dist[B]=dist[C]=dist[D]=∞. All unvisited.","Visit A (dist 0). Relax: dist[B]=min(∞,0+4)=4; dist[C]=min(∞,0+1)=1.","Visit C (dist 1, smallest unvisited). Relax: dist[B]=min(4,1+2)=3 (improved); dist[D]=min(∞,1+8)=9.","Visit B (dist 3, smallest unvisited). Relax: dist[D]=min(9,3+5)=8 (improved).","Visit D (dist 8) — no unvisited neighbors left."],"result":"Shortest distances from A: A=0, C=1, B=3 (via A→C→B, cheaper than the direct A→B edge of 4), D=8 (via A→C→B→D) — illustrating why Dijkstra must keep relaxing even after finding one path, since a shorter one can be found through an intermediate vertex."}}
{"title":"String Algorithms","summary":"String algorithms solve problems specific to sequences of characters, and pattern matching — finding all occurrences of a pattern of length m within a text of length n — is the foundational problem. The naive approach checks every starting position and compares character by character, giving O(nm) worst-case time; the Knuth-Morris-Pratt (KMP) algorithm improves this to O(n+m) by precomputing a failure function (partial match table) for the pattern, which records, for each prefix, the length of the longest proper prefix that is also a suffix of that prefix — this lets the algorithm avoid re-examining text characters already matched, sliding the pattern forward using the failure function instead of restarting from scratch after a mismatch. Rabin-Karp takes a different approach, using a rolling hash to compute a hash of each length-m window of the text in O(1) time per window (after an initial O(m) computation), comparing hashes first and only doing a full character comparison when hashes match (to rule out collisions), giving expected O(n+m) time, though its worst case remains O(nm) if many spurious hash collisions occur. Edit distance (Levenshtein distance) — the minimum number of single-character insertions, deletions, and substitutions to transform one string into another — is a classic DP problem, solved by a table where entry [i][j] represents the edit distance between the first i characters of one string and first j of the other, giving O(nm) time and space. Beyond individual algorithms, string problems frequently combine ideas from other areas — hashing (Rabin-Karp), dynamic programming (edit distance, longest common subsequence), and tries (efficient multi-pattern search) — which is why string algorithms are often where earlier topics get applied together.","learning_outcomes":["Trace the naive string matching algorithm on a small text and pattern, identifying why it re-examines characters after a mismatch.","Construct the KMP failure function (partial match table) for a given pattern, and use it to trace how KMP avoids redundant comparisons after a mismatch.","Explain the rolling hash technique used in Rabin-Karp, and state why its expected time is O(n+m) despite an O(nm) worst case."],"common_mistakes":["Assuming KMP's speedup comes from skipping characters in the text — it never re-examines a matched text character; the speedup comes from using the failure function to shift the pattern, not the text pointer, after a mismatch.","Miscomputing the failure function by confusing 'prefix' with 'substring' — it specifically requires a proper prefix that is also a suffix of the same substring, not just any matching substring elsewhere in the pattern.","Skipping the verification step in Rabin-Karp after a hash match — a hash match doesn't guarantee true equality (a collision is possible), so a full character comparison is still required before reporting a match.","Initializing edit distance's DP table incorrectly — the base-case row and column (transforming an empty string into a prefix via pure insertions/deletions) must be correct, since every other entry depends on it and errors propagate through the table."],"worked_example":{"setup":"Compute the KMP failure function (longest proper prefix that is also a suffix, for each prefix) for the pattern 'ABABAC'.","steps":["π[0] ('A'): no proper prefix exists. π[0]=0.","π[1] ('AB'): proper prefix 'A' vs suffix 'B' — no match. π[1]=0.","π[2] ('ABA'): 'AB' vs suffix 'BA' — no; 'A' vs suffix 'A' — match. π[2]=1.","π[3] ('ABAB'): 'ABA' vs suffix 'BAB' — no; 'AB' vs suffix 'AB' — match. π[3]=2.","π[4] ('ABABA'): 'ABAB' vs suffix 'BABA' — no; 'ABA' vs suffix 'ABA' — match. π[4]=3.","π[5] ('ABABAC'): checking all proper prefixes against corresponding suffixes — none match, since the pattern ends in 'C', which never appears earlier. π[5]=0."],"result":"Failure function for 'ABABAC': [0,0,1,2,3,0] — the rising 0,1,2,3 reflects the pattern's repeating 'AB' structure (each new 'AB' extends the previous match by one), while the final 0 reflects that 'C' breaks any overlap, since it never appears earlier in the pattern."}}
